  abstract={This article presents a fine-grained thermal sensing network for thermal management in SoCs. Sensor nodes in this network are made up of joint supply voltage ( $V_{\mathrm {dd}}$ ) and temperature ( $T$ ) sensors, which are compact and highly digital. Measurements from these simple but imperfect sensors are jointly processed to extract accurate  $T$  and  $V_{\mathrm {dd}}$  measurements across the network. This computationally enabled approach produces sensors with a small footprint, low  $V_{\text {dd}}$ -sensitivity, high supply-noise tolerance, and with accuracy and measurement times suitable for thermal management applications. Fabricated in 65-nm CMOS, a prototype network of eight sensors connected to a shared processing unit achieves a worst-case inaccuracy of  $+ 1.24~^{\circ } $ C/ $- 1.04~^{\circ } $ C ( $3\sigma $ ) across a 0.6–1.0 V  $V_{\mathrm {dd}}$  range and a  $0~^{\circ } $ C– $100~^{\circ } $ C temperature range. Each sensor node occupies  $67~\mu \text {m}^{2}$ .},
